Chapter 9 Q1: Explain how creating and using classes supports Object Oriented Programming and S.O.L.I.D. design principles, and give specific examples.
Creating and using classes is an important part of Object Oriented Programming because classes help organize related data and behaviors into one place. A class can represent a real world object and contain attributes that store information and methods that define what the object can do. For example, a Car class could store the model,make, and year of car and include methods that display information about it. The chapter also shows how inheritance can be used so a class like ElectricCar can resuse features from a regular Car class instead of rewriting the same code. Classes can also support S.O.L.I.D. design principles. One example is the Single Responsibility Principle, which means that a class should focus on one main responsibility. For example, instead of putting all battery related information inside of an ElectricCar class, a separate Battery class can handle battery information. This keeps each class more organized and easier to understand. The chapter demonstrates this by moving battery features into their own class and then using a Battery object inside ElectricCar. This makes programs easier to maintain, update, and reuse as they become larger. 
